{
 "meta": {"level": "A1", "section": "Verbs", "number": 2, "total_in_level": 9,
  "title": "zijn and hebben",
  "subtitle": "to be and to have: introducing yourself, age, origin and job, questions and negatives",
  "footer": "Language Learning · A1 · Verbs · zijn and hebben",
  "next": "Next: A1 · pdf 3 — The present tense"},
 "blocks": [
  {"type": "callout", "kind": "tip", "title": "After this PDF you can", "bullets": [
   "conjugate <b>zijn</b> (to be) and <b>hebben</b> (to have)",
   "use the subject pronouns, including <b>jij</b> and <b>u</b>",
   "introduce yourself: name, age, country, job",
   "say how you feel: <i>Ik heb honger, Ik ben moe</i>"]},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "1. The subject pronouns"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Many Dutch pronouns have a stressed and an unstressed form. In speech the unstressed form is normal: <i>Ben je moe?</i> The stressed form gives emphasis or contrast: <i>Ben <b>jij</b> moe? Ik niet.</i>"},
  {"type": "table", "style": "plain", "widths": [4.0, 4.0, 4.0, 5.0], "head": ["English", "Stressed", "Unstressed", "Note"], "rows": [
   ["I", "ik", "('k)", "the short form is only spoken"],
   ["you (informal)", "jij", "je", "friends, family, children, people your age"],
   ["you (polite)", "u", "u", "strangers, older people, formal situations"],
   ["he / she / it", "hij / zij / het", "(ie) / ze / 't", "zij = she, but also they"],
   ["we", "wij", "we", ""],
   ["you (plural)", "jullie", "jullie", "more than one person, informal"],
   ["they", "zij", "ze", ""]]},
  {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "jij or u?", "text": "English has one <i>you</i>; Dutch has two. Use <b>jij</b> with friends, family and people of your own age, and <b>u</b> with strangers, older people and in formal letters. The Dutch switch to jij quickly; if someone says <i>Zeg maar jij</i>, you may use jij."},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "2. zijn (to be)"},
  {"type": "conj", "verb": "zijn", "meaning": "to be", "label": "irregular verb", "tense": "present tense",
   "forms": ["ben", "bent", "is", "zijn", "zijn", "zijn"],
   "examples": [["Ik ben Engels.", "I'm English."], ["Jij bent te laat.", "You're late."], ["Zij is verpleegkundige.", "She's a nurse."], ["Wij zijn thuis.", "We're at home."], ["Jullie zijn heel aardig.", "You're very kind."], ["Zij zijn uit Schotland.", "They're from Scotland."]]},
  {"type": "callout", "kind": "warn", "title": "Ben je ...? The t disappears", "text": "When <b>jij / je</b> comes after the verb, the -t of the verb disappears: <i>Jij bent moe</i>, but <b>Ben je moe?</b> This rule works for almost every verb (pdf 3). With <b>u</b> you say <i>u bent</i> or, less often, <i>u is</i>."},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "3. hebben (to have)"},
  {"type": "conj", "verb": "hebben", "meaning": "to have", "label": "irregular verb", "tense": "present tense",
   "forms": ["heb", "hebt", "heeft", "hebben", "hebben", "hebben"],
   "examples": [["Ik heb twee broers.", "I've got two brothers."], ["Heb je een pen?", "Have you got a pen?"], ["Hij heeft een hond.", "He's got a dog."], ["Wij hebben een nieuwe auto.", "We've got a new car."], ["Jullie hebben veel boeken.", "You've got a lot of books."], ["Zij hebben drie kinderen.", "They've got three children."]]},
  {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Hunger, thirst and cold go with hebben", "bullets": [
   "<b>Ik heb honger / dorst</b> = I'm hungry / thirsty (literally: I have hunger).",
   "<b>Ik heb het koud / warm</b> = I'm cold / hot. <i>Ik ben koud</i> means that you are a cold person.",
   "<b>Ik heb gelijk</b> = I'm right. <b>Ik heb haast</b> = I'm in a hurry.",
   "But age goes with zijn: <b>Ik ben twintig (jaar)</b>, not <i>Ik heb twintig jaar</i>."]},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "4. Introducing yourself"},
  {"type": "table", "style": "plain", "widths": [3.6, 6.6, 6.8], "head": ["Topic", "Dutch", "English"], "rows": [
   ["Name", "Ik ben Sam. / Ik heet Sam.", "I'm Sam. / My name is Sam."],
   ["Age", "Ik ben vijfentwintig (jaar).", "I'm twenty-five."],
   ["Country", "Ik kom uit Engeland.", "I'm from England."],
   ["Nationality", "Ik ben Engels.", "I'm English."],
   ["Job", "Ik ben ingenieur.", "I'm an engineer."],
   ["Where you live", "Ik woon in Utrecht.", "I live in Utrecht."]]},
  {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "No article with a job", "text": "English says <i>I'm <b>a</b> teacher</i>. Dutch leaves the article out: <b>Ik ben leraar</b>, <b>Zij is arts</b>. With an adjective it comes back: <i>Zij is een goede arts.</i>"},
  {"type": "verbpairs", "title": "Countries and nationalities", "items": [
   ["Nederland", "the Netherlands"], ["Nederlands", "Dutch"], ["België", "Belgium"], ["Belgisch", "Belgian"],
   ["Engeland", "England"], ["Engels", "English"], ["het Verenigd Koninkrijk", "the United Kingdom"], ["Brits", "British"],
   ["Amerika", "America"], ["Amerikaans", "American"], ["Duitsland", "Germany"], ["Duits", "German"]]},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "5. Questions and negatives"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "For a yes/no question, put the verb first: <b>Ben je moe?</b> <b>Heeft zij een auto?</b> No <i>do</i> is needed. For a negative, use <b>niet</b> (not) with an adjective or a verb, and <b>geen</b> (no, not a) before a noun with <i>een</i> or without an article."},
  {"type": "table", "style": "plain", "widths": [5.6, 5.6, 5.8], "head": ["Question", "Yes", "No"], "rows": [
   ["Ben je moe?", "Ja, ik ben moe.", "Nee, ik ben niet moe."],
   ["Is hij Engels?", "Ja, hij is Engels.", "Nee, hij is niet Engels."],
   ["Heb je een auto?", "Ja, ik heb een auto.", "Nee, ik heb geen auto."],
   ["Heeft zij kinderen?", "Ja, ze heeft twee kinderen.", "Nee, ze heeft geen kinderen."]]},
  {"type": "verbpairs", "title": "Useful questions", "items": [
   ["Hoe heet je?", "What's your name?"], ["Waar kom je vandaan?", "Where are you from?"],
   ["Hoe oud ben je?", "How old are you?"], ["Hoe gaat het?", "How are you?"],
   ["Wie is dat?", "Who's that?"], ["Waar woon je?", "Where do you live?"]]},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "6. Common mistakes"},
  {"type": "mistakes", "rows": [
   ["Ik ben honger.", "Ik heb honger.", "Hunger and thirst go with hebben."],
   ["Ik heb twintig jaar.", "Ik ben twintig jaar.", "Age goes with zijn."],
   ["Ik ben een leraar.", "Ik ben leraar.", "No article with a job."],
   ["Bent je moe?", "Ben je moe?", "After the verb, jij loses the -t."],
   ["Hij hebt een hond.", "Hij heeft een hond.", "hij, zij, het: heeft."],
   ["Ik heb niet een auto.", "Ik heb geen auto.", "Before a noun with een: geen."],
   ["Ik ben koud.", "Ik heb het koud.", "Feeling cold: het koud hebben."]]},
  {"type": "h1", "text": "7. Self-check"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Fill in the right form of zijn or hebben, or niet or geen. The answers are on the last page."},
  {"type": "exercises", "items": [
   {"q": "Ik ______ uit Londen.", "hint": "zijn", "a": "ben", "full": "Ik ben uit Londen."},
   {"q": "Zij ______ verpleegkundige.", "hint": "zijn", "a": "is", "full": "Zij is verpleegkundige."},
   {"q": "______ je moe?", "hint": "zijn, question", "a": "ben", "full": "Ben je moe?"},
   {"q": "Wij ______ een hond.", "hint": "hebben", "a": "hebben", "full": "Wij hebben een hond."},
   {"q": "Hij ______ twee zussen.", "hint": "hebben", "a": "heeft", "full": "Hij heeft twee zussen."},
   {"q": "Ik heb ______. Is er eten?", "hint": "hungry", "a": "honger", "full": "Ik heb honger. Is er eten?"},
   {"q": "Mijn broer ______ dertig jaar.", "hint": "zijn", "a": "is", "full": "Mijn broer is dertig jaar."},
   {"q": "Ik heb ______ auto.", "hint": "no car", "a": "geen", "full": "Ik heb geen auto."},
   {"q": "Ik ben ______ moe.", "hint": "not tired", "a": "niet", "full": "Ik ben niet moe."},
   {"q": "______ u een vraag?", "hint": "hebben, question, polite", "a": "heeft", "full": "Heeft u een vraag?"},
   {"q": "Waar kom je ______?", "hint": "from", "a": "vandaan", "full": "Waar kom je vandaan?"},
   {"q": "Jullie ______ heel aardig.", "hint": "zijn", "a": "zijn", "full": "Jullie zijn heel aardig."}]},
  {"type": "p", "text": "<b>Translate into Dutch:</b>"},
  {"type": "translations", "items": [
   {"q": "I'm twenty-five.", "a": "Ik ben vijfentwintig."},
   {"q": "She is a teacher.", "a": "Zij is lerares."},
   {"q": "Where are you from?", "a": "Waar kom je vandaan?"},
   {"q": "We are cold.", "a": "We hebben het koud."}]},
  {"type": "pagebreak"},
  {"type": "answers"}
 ]
}
